.TH BSKY 1
.SH NAME
bsky \- Bluesky client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bsky
.I credfile
.RB [ timeline
.RB [ -n
.IR nnn ]
.RB [ -c
.IR cursor ]
.RB [ -v ]]
.br
.B bsky
.I credfile
.B post
.IR text\ ...
.br
.B bsky
.I credfile
.B reply
.I uri cid
.IR text\ ...
.br
.B bsky
.I credfile
.B like
.I uri cid
.br
.B bsky
.I credfile
.B repost
.I uri cid
.br
.B bsky
.I credfile
.B follow
.I handle-or-did
.br
.B bsky
.I credfile
.B notifications
.RB [ -n
.IR nnn ]
.br
.B bsky
.I credfile
.B profile
.RI [ actor ]
.br
.B bsky
.I credfile
.B thread
.I uri
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Bsky
is a Bluesky (AT Protocol) client. Every invocation logs in to
.B bsky.social
with the handle and app password read from
.IR credfile ,
then runs one command;
.B timeline
is the default.
.PP
.B Timeline
prints the home timeline, one line per post, oldest last:
.EX
\&@alice.bsky.social: what a nice day
\&@bob.example.com: shipped a thing
.EE
.B -n
sets how many posts (1\-100, default 30);
.B -v
follows each post with an indented line carrying its
.I uri
and
.I cid
\(em the two values
.BR reply ,
.B like
and
.B repost
take. When more posts remain, the last line names the
.B -c
.I cursor
that continues where this page stopped.
.PP
.B Post
publishes
.I text
(the remaining arguments, joined with spaces) as a new post and
prints its
.B at://
URI.
.B Reply
posts under the post named by
.I uri
and
.IR cid ,
first fetching that post to find the thread's root, so replies deep
in a thread attach correctly.
.B Like
and
.B repost
act on the post so named.
.B Follow
takes a handle (resolved to a DID via the server) or a literal
.BI did: ...
identifier. Every one of these writes is a single
.B com.atproto.repo.createRecord
call \(em AT Protocol represents posts, likes, reposts and follows
all as records in the caller's own repository.
.PP
.B Notifications
lists recent notifications, one per line: the reason
.RB ( like ,
.BR repost ,
.BR follow ,
.BR mention ,
.BR reply ,
.BR quote ),
who, and the text when the notification carries a post.
.B Profile
prints
.IR actor 's
profile (the caller's own when
.I actor
is omitted): handle, DID, display name, description, and counts.
.B Thread
prints the thread around the post named by
.IR uri ,
ancestors first, replies indented beneath their parents.
.PP
.I Credfile
holds exactly two lines: the account handle, then an app password
\(em never the real account password. An app password is generated
from Bluesky's own Settings > App Passwords page, and can be revoked
independently of the main login. Keep this file readable only by its
owner; nothing here enforces that.
.B Bsky
never places the password on a command line (where a
.IR ps (1)
listing could see it) or in any diagnostic output.
.SH TRANSPORT
.B Bsky
speaks HTTPS through
.IR https (2),
this tree's own HTTP-over-TLS-1.2 client
.RI ( tls12 (2),
which verifies the server's certificate chain against the tree's
trust roots). It originally drove the host's
.IR curl (1)
through
.B #C
because
.B ssl3
.RI ( module/ssl3.m )
is SSLv3-era and cannot reach a modern HTTPS host; that stopgap is
gone. Name resolution falls back to the hosted
.IR emu 's
built-in resolver when no connection server serves
.IR /net/cs ,
so hosted mode remains the tested configuration.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/bsky.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR bskytest (1),
.IR masto (1),
.IR https (2),
.IR tls12 (2)
.SH BUGS
One network; no feeds other than the home timeline, no search, no
media (posting or viewing), no quote posts, no unlike/unrepost/
unfollow (deleting a record is a different call this does not make),
no muting or blocking, and no GUI. Each invocation is a fresh login
\(em there is no session caching, so very frequent invocations can
meet
.BR bsky.social 's
rate limits. The write commands were verified against the real
server only up to authentication (a bogus token draws the server's
own decoded error); actually publishing posts from a test run to a
real account would be spam, so the final createRecord acceptance
path relies on the pinned record shapes in
.B buildtest()
matching the lexicon.
